+## Agent callers and per-agent delegation (FK-16, 0.3.32)
+
+With `authority.local`, an agent caller (an `app.agent` run, an agent session such as an MCP client) asks
+AuthBoundry **as itself**: only what a person granted *that agent* allows it. That needs an
+`@authboundry/local` with per-agent delegation (`registerAgent`). The only published release, 0.1.0, has none.
+
+- **Default: fail closed.** Without per-agent delegation installed, every decision by an agent caller is
+  `unavailable`. People's and services' decisions are unaffected. FeltKit does not fall back to the executor
+  on its own: that would let every agent of the application use every grant people gave the executor.
+- **Opt-in: `authority: { local: { agentCallers: "executor-grants" } }`.** The application owner may choose the
+  model FeltKit had through 0.3.30: an agent caller that names no agent is decided as the executor, with the
+  executor's grants. **This widens authority** and is not equivalent to per-agent delegation: one person's
+  grant to the application is usable by every agent. Each such decision carries
+  `compatibility: "executor-grants"` (decision and activity record), and readiness warns
+  (`authority.agent-callers`), so operators see it without reading logs. Invalid values fail when the app is
+  defined.
+- **Never widened by the opt-in:** a decision that names an agent stays `unavailable`; an agent can never decide
+  as another agent; grants naming an agent and `ctx.authority.agents` stay refused without per-agent delegation.
+- **Once per-agent delegation is installed** the setting has no effect (readiness says so): agents decide as
+  themselves. To move: install it, register agents (an operator's act; `app.agent`s register at start), have
+  people grant each agent (`grants.grant({ agent })`), then remove `agentCallers`.
+
